<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Lollius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 2 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Lollius was a Roman politician and general, consul in 21 BCE and a close associate of Augustus. In 16 BCE, as governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, he was defeated by the Sugambri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usipetes">Usipetes</a> and Tencteri, who captured the eagle of the Fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, a disaster known as the clades Lolliana. He later advised Augustus's grandson Gaius Caesar in the East.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-lollius.jpg" alt="Marcus Lollius" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlomorino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ponte_fabricio03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lollius belonged to the plebeian gens Lollia and was probably a new man in politics. He has been identified with the Marcus whom Appian describes as a legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a>, proscribed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a> in 42 BCE, who hid as a slave until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> persuaded Octavian to remove his name from the lists. He fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Battle of Actium</a> in 31 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 25 BCE Augustus made him the first Roman governor of Galatia, formerly a kingdom whose last king, Amyntas, had died. Lollius brought Amyntas's army into the Roman army and founded a Roman colony in the province. He was consul in 21 BCE with his old friend Quintus Aemilius Lepidus, the first of his gens to hold the office, and their names stand on the Pons Fabricius in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, whose repairs they approved. As governor of Macedonia in 19/18 BCE he defeated the Thracian Bersi.</p><p>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> his legions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> were defeated by the Sicambri, Tencteri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usipetes">Usipetes</a>. Suetonius, pairing the clades Lolliana with the disaster of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>, called it disgraceful rather than dangerous. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> was sent in 15 BCE to recover the standard, and Lollius never again commanded an army, though he stayed on friendly terms with Augustus. In 2/1 BCE Augustus made him tutor to Gaius Caesar on his mission to the East. Lollius was blamed for turning Gaius against Tiberius and accused of taking bribes from the Parthian king Phraates; according to different sources he then poisoned himself, killed himself in some other way, or died of natural causes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called Lollius a hypocrite who cared only for wealth, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velleius-paterculus">Velleius Paterculus</a>, a partisan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, described him as greedy and corrupt, while his friend the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> praised him as reliable and above avarice. His fortune passed to his granddaughter Lollia Paulina, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>. At Sagalassos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, excavations in 2005 and 2006 found the base of a colossal statue honouring him as the city's patron; its remains are in the Burdur Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcus Lollius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 16 BCE they crossed the Rhine with the Usipetes and Tencteri and defeated the Roman legate <b>Marcus Lollius</b>, capturing the eagle of the Fifth Legion, an event known as the clades Lolliana.</blockquote><a href="/state/sugambri">Sugambri</a></li><li><blockquote>In 16 BCE they joined the Sugambri in defeating <b>Marcus Lollius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tencteri">Tencteri</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferentino">Ferentino</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.68,13.25&amp;year=-8" aria-label="Ferentino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marcus Lollius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5323 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5323 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.68,13.25&amp;year=-8">What was here in 8 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
